General. All manipulations involving air-sensitive materials were performed in a Vacuum Atmospheres glovebox \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\), \(0.5\ \mathrm{ppm}\). Toluene and THF were dried by distillation from Na/benzophenone outside the glovebox and again inside the box prior to use. Toluene- \(d_{8}\) was dried over molecular sieves and filtered through a \(0.2\;\mu\) syringe filter prior to use. "Jones reductor" \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{Hg})_{2}\) was prepared by treating Zn pellets with dilute \(\mathrm{HgCl}_{2}\) solution, washing with dilute hydrochloric acid then water, and drying under vacuum. \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\)-enriched \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) \((10{-}15\%)\) was purchased from MER Corp. Meso-3-pyridyl-triphenylporphyrin and the \(\mathrm{PdCl}_{2}\)-linked dimer, \(\mathrm{H}_{4}\mathrm{JawsP}\), were prepared as previously described.\(^{30}\) \(\mathrm{C}_{120}\mathrm{O}^{55,56}\) and \(N\)-methylpyrrolidine-derivatized \(\mathrm{C}_{60}{}^{57}\) were prepared by literature methods. Host–guest complexes were prepared by additions of fullerenes and porphyrins in toluene. NMR spectra were recorded in toluene- \(d_{8}\) on an Inova \(500\,\mathrm{MHz}\) spectrometer and internally referenced to residual protio peaks of the solvent. MALDI TOF mass spectra were recorded on a PerSeptive Biosystems Voyager-DE STR instrument operated in linear mode with laser intensity of ca. 2000 eV. Air-stable samples were mixed with dithronol matrix. Air-sensitive samples were used directly, loading under anaerobic conditions. Titrations were performed with \(10^{-3}\mathrm{~M~}\) solutions of porphyrins and incremental additions of \(10^{-3}\,\mathrm{M}\) solutions of \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\)-enriched \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) dissolved in the porphyrin stock solution. Binding constants were determined from the variation in \({}^{13}\mathrm{C}\) NMR shifts using the WinEQNMR program.\(^{58}\)
Molecular Modeling. Host/guest complexes were constructed using the program CERIUS\(^{52}\).\(^{59}\) Geometry optimization was carried out using the universal force field.\(^{60}\) Gas-phase binding energies were estimated by comparison of the energy of the porphyrin-fullerene complex with the optimized energies of \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) and the bis-porphyrin. In this model, the gas-phase interaction energy of a single porphyrin with \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) is between 28 and \(30\ \mathrm{kcal\ mol^{-1}}\) depending on the porphyrin substituents. Bis-porphyrins that give effective fullerene binding are found to have about twice this energy, while those with weaker binding are less. The main feature contributing to weaker binding is the strain imposed on some hosts upon complexation. Semiempirical MO calculations using the PM3 method\(^{45}\) were carried out using SPARTAN (Version 5.1, Wave function Inc.). A Mulliken population analysis was performed on the PM3 optimized structure to estimate the atomic charge distribution on the fullerene.
