X-ray Structure Determinations. \(\mathbf{Cu}_{2}(\mathbf{JawsP})\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{C}_{60}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{1}\) 5hexane. Black crystals were grown by layered diffusion of hexanes into a toluene solution of 1:1 \(\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(\mathrm{JawsP})\) and \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) . A fragment of a thin needle (0.27 \(\times\ 0.21\,\times\,0.04\ \mathrm{mm}^{3})\) ) was used. The crystal was mounted onto a glass fiber and coated with epoxy resin to prevent the loss of solvent of crystallization. X-ray intensity data were collected at 213(2) K with the CCD detector placed at a distance of \(4.8450~\mathrm{cm}\) from the crystal. On the basis of a orthorhombic crystal system, the integrated frames yielded a total of 93 497 reflections at a maximum \(2\theta\) angle of \(43.94^{\circ}\) \((0.95\,\mathrm{\AA}\) resolution), of which 10 882 were independent reflections \((R_{\mathrm{int}}\) \(=0.0996\) , \(R_{\mathrm{sig}}=0.0722\) , redundancy \(=8.6\) , completeness \(=100\%\) ) and 6566 \((60.3\%)\) reflections were greater than \(2\sigma(I)\) . The unit cell parameters were \(a=27.903(2)\) \(\mathrm{A},b=43.578(3)\,\mathrm{\AA},c=29.242(2)\,\mathrm{\AA}\) , \(\alpha=90^{\circ}\) , \(\beta=90^{\circ}\) , \(\gamma=90^{\circ}\) , \(V=35\,556(5)\,\mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) , \(Z=8\) , calculated density \(D_{\mathrm{c}}~=~1.190~\mathrm{\g/cm}^{3}\) . Absorption corrections were applied (absorption coefficient \(\mu\,=\,0.424\,\mathrm{\mm^{-1}})\) ) to the raw intensity data using the SADABS program in the SAINTPLUS software.62 The maximum/minimum transmission factors were 0.9833/0.8942. The Bruker SHELXTL (Version 5.1) software package63 was used for phase determination and structure refinement. The distribution of intensities
\((E^{2}\mathrm{~-~}1\mathrm{~=~}0.936)\) and systematic absent reflections indicated one possible space group, Ccca. The space group Ccca was later determined to be correct. Patterson method was used to locate the Pd and Cu atoms. Subsequent isotropic least-squares refinements led to an electron density map from which all of the non-hydrogen atoms were identified in the asymmetry unit of the unit cell, including the four partially occupied hexane solvent molecules. The site occupancy factors for the four hexane molecules were 50, 47, 36, and \(17\%\) , which gave a total of 1.5 hexane molecules in the asymmetry unit. The \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) , the four partially occupied hexane molecules, and the nine disordered methyl groups were modeled as rigid groups using the EQIV, SAIDI, DFIX, DELU, SIMU, ISOR commands. The \(\mathrm{C}_{60}\) and the Pd1 atom were located on a 2-fold rotation axis \((-x,y,1.5-z\) symmetry operator). Atomic coordinates and isotropic and anisotropic displacement parameters of all the non-hydrogen atoms were refined by means of a full matrix least-squares procedure on \(F^{2}\) . All H-atoms were included in the refinement in calculated positions riding on the atoms to which they were attached. The refinement converged at \(\mathrm{R}1=0.0598\) , \(\mathrm{wR}2=0.1638\) , with \(I>\) \(2\sigma(I)\) . The largest peak/hole in the final difference map was \(1.088/-\) \(0.791\;\mathrm{e}/\mathrm{\AA}^{3}\) .
